## Why This Question Is Asked

This question evaluates the ability to design crash-safe, lock-free data structures for non-volatile memory systems. It tests understanding of durability guarantees, memory ordering semantics, and recovery mechanisms.

## Key Concepts

- **Durable memory semantics** in NVRAM environments
- **Log-structured queue design** for write optimization
- **Per-producer segmentation** to reduce contention
- **Atomic tail descriptors** and sequence counters to mitigate ABA problems
- **Recovery replay** mechanisms and correctness guarantees

## Code Example

```c
typedef struct {
    volatile uint64_t head;
    volatile uint64_t tail;
    _Atomic uint64_t durable_tail;
    void *segments[NUM_SEGMENTS];
} NVRAMQueue;

// Pseudo-code for enqueue
int enqueue(NVRAMQueue *q, void *data) {
    uint64_t segment = get_segment_id();
    uint64_t idx = atomic_fetch_add(&q->tail, 1) % SEGMENT_SIZE;

    // Write data and flush to NVRAM
    write_and_flush(&q->segments[segment][idx], data);

    // Memory barrier before advancing durable tail
    atomic_thread_fence(memory_order_release);

    // Atomically advance durable tail
    uint64_t old = atomic_load(&q->durable_tail);
    atomic_compare_exchange_strong(&q->durable_tail, &old, old + 1);
    return 0;
}

// Pseudo-code for dequeue with recovery safety
void *dequeue(NVRAMQueue *q) {
    uint64_t durable = atomic_load(&q->durable_tail);
    uint64_t current = atomic_load(&q->head);

    if (current >= durable) return NULL; // Nothing to dequeue

    // Read with acquire barrier
    atomic_thread_fence(memory_order_acquire);

    return read_and_invalidate(&q->segments[current % SEGMENT_SIZE]);
}

// Recovery procedure
int recover(NVRAMQueue *q) {
    uint64_t durable = atomic_load(&q->durable_tail);
    uint64_t head = atomic_load(&q->head);

    // Replay log to find last consistent state
    while (head < durable) {
        validate_and_replay_entry(q, head);
        head++;
    }

    atomic_store(&q->head, durable);
    return 0;
}
```

## Discussion Points

### Memory Ordering Barriers
- Use `memory_order_release` before advancing the durable tail to ensure all writes are visible
- Use `memory_order_acquire` when reading the tail to synchronize with previous producers

### Cache-Line Alignment
- Align each segment to cache-line boundaries (typically 64 bytes) to prevent false sharing
- Pad metadata structures to avoid contention between producers

### Failure Safety
- The durable tail only advances after NVRAM flushes complete
- Recovery replays from the last known head position to the durable tail
- Checkpoint periodically to bound recovery time

### Microbenchmark Plan
1. **Mixed contention**: Run multiple producers/consumers with varying workloads
2. **Simulated power loss**: Use fsync/fence operations to model durability
3. **Metrics**: Measure throughput, latency, and recovery time after crash
4. **Test scenarios**: Single producer, multiple producers, high contention, and crash-recovery sequences

## Why This Is Asked
Tests knowledge of non-blocking data structures, ABA avoidance, memory ordering, and NUMA-aware performance under mixed-priority traffic. It also touches on backpressure and practical microbenchmark design.

## Key Concepts
- Lock-free design and ABA avoidance with per-slot sequence counters
- Memory ordering: release on enqueue, acquire on dequeue
- NUMA-aware layout and cache-line alignment
- Priority routing, backpressure metrics, and spillover handling
- Correct wrap-around handling and bounded capacity

## Code Example
```javascript
// Ring buffer with 64-bit sequence per slot
struct Slot {
    alignas(64) uint8_t data[64];
    std::atomic<uint64_t> seq;
};

class Ring {
    Slot buffer[N];
    std::atomic<uint64_t> head{0}, tail{0};

public:
    bool enqueue(const void* payload) {
        uint64_t pos = tail.fetch_add(1, std::memory_order_relaxed);
        uint64_t seq = buffer[pos & mask].seq.load(std::memory_order_acquire);
        if (seq != pos) return false; // full or wrap-around
        memcpy(buffer[pos & mask].data, payload, 64);
        buffer[pos & mask].seq.store(pos + 1, std::memory_order_release);
        return true;
    }

    bool dequeue(void* payload) {
        uint64_t pos = head.load(std::memory_order_relaxed);
        uint64_t seq = buffer[pos & mask].seq.load(std::memory_order_acquire);
        if (seq != pos + 1) return false; // empty
        memcpy(payload, buffer[pos & mask].data, 64);
        buffer[pos & mask].seq.store(pos + N + 1, std::memory_order_release);
        head.store(pos + 1, std::memory_order_relaxed);
        return true;
    }
};

// Two rings per priority, producer selects based on flag
```

## Performance Measurement
- **Latency**: Use RDTSC/RDTSCP around enqueue/dequeue, measure percentiles under mixed priority ratios (90/10, 50/50)
- **Backpressure**: Count enqueue failures per ring, track queue depth, measure producer stall time
- **NUMA**: Pin threads to sockets, allocate buffers locally, measure remote memory penalties
- **Throughput**: Vary producer/consumer ratios, monitor cache miss rates with perf

## Why This Is Asked
Tests fundamental low-level memory management, bit-twiddling, and safety in a confined arena.

## Key Concepts
- Bitmap-based allocators
- Alignment and bounds checks
- Safety against double-free

## Code Example
```c
#include <stdint.h>
#include <stddef.h>

typedef struct { uint64_t m0, m1; unsigned char* data; size_t blocks; } mem8_pool;

void mem8_init(mem8_pool* p, void* arena, size_t size){ p->data=(unsigned char*)arena+16; p->blocks=(size-16)/8; p->m0=0; p->m1=0; }

void* mem8_alloc(mem8_pool* p){ for(int i=0;i<128;i++){ int word = i/64; int bit = i%64; uint64_t mask = (1ULL<<bit); uint64_t* w = (word==0)? &p->m0 : &p->m1; if(!(*w & mask)){ *w |= mask; return p->data + i*8; } } return NULL; }

void mem8_free(mem8_pool* p, void* ptr){ size_t idx = ((unsigned char*)ptr - p->data)/8; if(idx< p->blocks){ int word = idx/64; int bit = idx%64; uint64_t mask = (1ULL<<bit); uint64_t* w = (word==0)? &p->m0 : &p->m1; *w &= ~mask; } }
```

